{
  "result": "At dawn, the city belongs to delivery trucks, pigeons, and people who have not yet decided what kind of day they are having. Windows brighten one by one, like cautious thoughts. A baker lifts a metal shutter; somewhere above him, a violinist practices the same four measures and stops before the difficult part.\n\nI like this unfinished hour. Nothing has settled into its official shape. The caf\u00e9 is not crowded, the sidewalks have room for wandering, and every doorway seems capable of offering a different future. Even familiar objects recover a little mystery: a bicycle chained to a fence, a glove on a bench, steam rising from a street grate as if the earth were thinking aloud.\n\nLater, schedules will harden. Messages will arrive with exclamation marks. Elevators will fill with perfume, rain, impatience, and small talk. We will become useful versions of ourselves, answering questions, moving chairs, buying groceries, remembering passwords. This is necessary, but necessity can be noisy.\n\nSo I keep a private allegiance to beginnings: the blank page, the first sip, the moment before a train moves. Beginnings do not promise success. They offer something gentler: the permission to proceed without knowing.\n\nPerhaps that is enough wisdom for an ordinary morning. Open the curtain. Water the plant. Put on shoes. Step outside before certainty wakes. Let the day remain unnamed for another minute, wide and bright at the edges, while you enter it not as a hero or a plan, but simply as a living question mark.",
